+## P25 and NXDN through a hotspot
+
+- **P25 shows a caller without audio.** Match the hotspot's NAC and reflector talkgroup. On a channel with selective
+  squelch, press **Monitor** during a clear call to try receiving its group. The `p25.played` field shows whether
+  the firmware selected the call for playback. If selected, compare the `p25.voice_bad`, `p25v.frames`, and
+  `p25v.peak` readings before and after speech. This distinguishes filtering, damaged voice frames and a
+  problem after decoding. [WPSD P25](https://manual.wpsd.radio/modes/p25/)
+- **The hotspot drops the P25 reflector at the end of an over.** Keep the MMDVMHost log from the RF start through
+  the end, including BER, and the gateway log around the disconnect. A conventional group call ends with a basic
+  P25 terminator that has no destination field. The RF and gateway records help locate where a different
+  destination appeared. Try the WPSD P25 Manager's **10 - Parrot** with a group contact for TG 10.
+- **The hotspot shows no NXDN RF call.** Match the modem's NXDN rate and the channel's TX RAN. Set the radio's
+  assigned NXDN unit ID in the CPS, separately from its DMR ID. Match the reflector group to the channel and
+  check the MMDVMHost RF log. [WPSD NXDN](https://manual.wpsd.radio/modes/nxdn/)
+
+For weak or intermittent decode, compare the modem's known RX/TX offsets and a measured carrier offset before
+changing [Freq trim](17-settings.md#advanced-last-row). Include the radio and modem firmware versions, receive and transmit
+frequencies, mode and rate, NAC or RAN, talkgroup, and whether DMR worked through the same modem. With the programming
+cable, `tools/rfoundry_debug.py view --section p25` or `--section nxdn` reads the firmware counters.
+
